Recommended briefing bullets:
• Resident savings: reduces household energy bills and increases comfort through proven efficiency upgrades;
• Climate benefits: accelerates city progress toward emissions targets by lowering building energy use;
• Equity focus: prioritize low- and moderate-income households to deliver direct financial relief where it’s needed most;
• Local jobs and supply chain: creates steady demand for contractors, auditors, and suppliers, supporting small businesses and workforce development;
• Grid and resilience benefits: lowers peak demand, eases strain on the grid, and improves building resilience to extreme heat/cold;
• Leverage and multiplier effects: city rebates unlock state/federal incentives and private financing, stretching public dollars further;
• Implementation realism: uptake has been slower than projected—expand modestly while funding targeted outreach, streamlined applications, and performance tracking to improve cost-effectiveness and program responsiveness

Q1 — Why expand a program that hasn’t hit participation targets? A1 — The program’s objectives remain sound: it reduces household energy bills, advances our emissions goals, and generates local work for contractors and suppliers, and a modest expansion paired with targeted fixes is the pragmatic step to capture those benefits;  
Q2 — What are the primary advantages we’re selling to residents and neighborhoods? A2 — Clear, practical wins — lower utility costs and improved comfort for households, measurable progress on city climate targets, and steadier demand for local small businesses and workforce development.

Q3 — Budget says cost‑effectiveness is unclear because uptake was slow; shouldn’t we pause? A3 — We take that concern seriously, but slower uptake is a signal to refine program design — better outreach to priority communities, streamlined applications, and targeted incentives — not an automatic reason to stop an initiative that delivers multiple co‑benefits;  
Q4 — How will we address the small‑landlord complaint that the paperwork is a hassle? A4 — We will simplify enrollment pathways for landlords, offer landlord‑focused guidance and batch processing options through trusted intermediaries, and explore contractor‑led enrollment to reduce admin burden.

Q5 — How will we boost participation in practice? A5 — By funding focused outreach partnerships with community groups, aligning rebate timing with contractor capacity, simplifying documentation, and piloting neighborhood outreach to test what works;  
Q6 — How will success be measured and who reports it? A6 — We will track uptake, average incentive per project, geographic and income distribution, and contractor participation on short feedback cycles and deliver monthly briefings so staff can tweak design quickly.

Q7 — What’s the fiscal risk of expanding now? A7 — We recommend a modest, staged increase so exposure is limited while we improve uptake and cost‑effectiveness, with the option to reallocate funds if metrics don’t trend as expected;  
Q8 — What does staff need from the Mayor to move forward? A8 — Approval for a modest budget increase, a green light on the outreach and simplification plan, and a commitment to receive early results so we can adapt the program in real time.
